RSI Five Layer Architecture 

The Five-Layer Definitions



Evolution carriers run from the outside in – lighter at the top, heavier at the bottom

Generality and on-device point to the same answer – move up the latter (the main battleground of self-evolution lies in L1-L3) 

        - The generality requires evolution artifacts to be transferable(textualized context policies, memories and skills can be carried across models & devices) 

        - The on-device requires zero or low parameter changes, artifacts stored as text, and private data staying inside the device. 







Layer | Managers (Lifespan) | What Evolves | On-Device | Generality

① L1 Context | Current Context (1 Episode)|The model actively manages its context (write/delete/compress),zero parameters changes | ★★★★★ |High: fully decoupled from the model

② L2 Memory / Knowledge | Knowledge & experience(Cross-task) |Experience externalized as portable knowledge (Markdown/vectors/KG) | ★★★★★ |High: artifacts transferable across models

③ L3 Skills | Ways of doing things (Cross-task/model) | Knowledge consolidated into executable skills and tools, reusable across tasks | ★★★ |Medium-high: skill libraries are portable

④ L4 Signals | Standards of “good”(/)| Source of the learning signal:Human reward  -> self-reward -> reward-free | ★★|Critical: reward-free is the key to generality

⑤ L5 Parameters | Model itself (As long as Model lives)| Weight-level updates | ★|Low: bound to a single model, with catastrophic forgetting



